{"id":"tag:blogger.com,1999:blog-8233516149842295530.post-5039872931638827919","year":"2025","month":"11","slug":"gram-schmidt-process","path":"/2025/11/gram-schmidt-process.html","title":"Gram-Schmidt Process Explained (The Math of QR Decomposition)","published":"2025-11-13T15:30:00.001Z","updated":"2025-11-13T15:30:00.117Z","labels":["Machine Learning","Mathematics"],"excerpt":"A developer's guide to the Gram-Schmidt Process. Learn how this algorithm uses projections to create orthonormal bases and powers QR Decomposition.","readingMinutes":4,"youtubeId":"8mRp7s3ClxQ","youtubeThumb":true,"coverImage":null,"headings":[{"id":"the-purification-algorithm-a-developer-s-guide-to-the-gram-schmidt-process","text":"The \"Purification\" Algorithm: A Developer's Guide to the Gram-Schmidt Process","level":2},{"id":"the-mental-model-purification-by-projection","text":"The Mental Model: Purification by Projection","level":2},{"id":"the-algorithm-step-by-step","text":"The Algorithm Step-by-Step","level":2},{"id":"the-payoff-qr-decomposition","text":"The Payoff: QR Decomposition","level":2},{"id":"in-code-the-numpy-way","text":"In Code: The NumPy Way","level":2},{"id":"conclusion","text":"Conclusion","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your Turn...","level":3}],"html":"<article>\n  <h2 id=\"the-purification-algorithm-a-developer-s-guide-to-the-gram-schmidt-process\">\n    The \"Purification\" Algorithm: A Developer's Guide to the Gram-Schmidt Process\n  </h2>\n  <div>\n    <p>In linear algebra, we often start with a basis for our vector space. But as we've discussed, not all bases are created equal. A \"messy\" basis—where vectors are skewed and not at 90-degree angles—can make calculations inefficient and numerically unstable. This is a real problem for engineers and data scientists who need reliable and fast computations.</p>\n    <p>What if there was an algorithm that could systematically take any messy, \"contaminated\" toolkit of vectors and \"purify\" it into a perfect, 90-degree, orthonormal one? There is. It's an elegant and powerful procedure called the <b>Gram-Schmidt Process</b>.</p>\n    <p>In this post, we'll decode this algorithm not as a formula to be memorized, but as an intuitive, step-by-step process of cleaning our data's toolkit.</p>\n    <p>Watch the video for the full visual story, then scroll down for the detailed breakdown and code.</p>\n    <div>\n      <div class=\"video-embed\"><iframe allow=\"accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share\" allowfullscreen=\"\" referrerpolicy=\"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/8mRp7s3ClxQ?si=_cfiO-ANk_rAgLnj\" title=\"YouTube video player\" loading=\"lazy\"></iframe></div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n\n  <h2 id=\"the-mental-model-purification-by-projection\">The Mental Model: Purification by Projection</h2>\n  <div>\n    <p>The intuition behind Gram-Schmidt is to build our clean, orthogonal basis one vector at a time. Let's say we start with a messy basis {<b>v₁</b>, <b>v₂</b>}. We want to produce a clean, orthogonal basis {<b>u₁</b>, <b>u₂</b>}.</p>\n    <p><b>Step 1 is easy:</b> We have nothing to compare against yet, so we accept the first vector <b>v₁</b> as the foundation for our new basis. We'll call it <b>u₁</b>.</p>\n    <p><b>Step 2 is the crucial insight:</b> The second vector, <b>v₂</b>, is \"contaminated.\" It contains a component that is aligned with <b>u₁</b>, and a \"pure\" component that is new and orthogonal to <b>u₁</b>. Our goal is to isolate this pure part.</p>\n    <p>To do this, we use the tool we mastered in the last video: the <b>Orthogonal Projection</b>. We can find the \"contaminated\" part of <b>v₂</b> by projecting it onto <b>u₁</b>. This projection is the \"shadow\" of <b>v₂</b> that lies along <b>u₁</b>.</p>\n    <p>Once we have this shadow, we can \"purify\" <b>v₂</b> by surgically subtracting the shadow from it. What's left over is the new, clean vector <b>u₂</b>, which is guaranteed to be orthogonal to <b>u₁</b>.</p>\n  </div>\n  \n  <h2 id=\"the-algorithm-step-by-step\">The Algorithm Step-by-Step</h2>\n  <div>\n    <p>Let's formalize this intuitive process. Given a starting basis {<b>v₁</b>, <b>v₂</b>, ..., <b>vₙ</b>}, we find the orthogonal basis {<b>u₁</b>, <b>u₂</b>, ..., <b>uₙ</b>} as follows:</p>\n    <ol>\n      <li><b>Find u₁:</b> Trivial. <span class=\"math-inline\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_1 = v_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.5806em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.5806em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">1</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></li>\n      <li><b>Find u₂:</b> Subtract the projection of <b>v₂</b> onto <b>u₁</b>.\n        <div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"katex-display\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>proj</mtext><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⋅</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u_2 = v_2 - \\text{proj}_{u_1}(v_2) = v_2 - \\left(\\frac{v_2 \\cdot u_1}{u_1 \\cdot u_1}\\right) u_1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.5806em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">u</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:0em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">v</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3011em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0359em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">2</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:1.0942em;vertical-align:-0.3442em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord text\"><span class=\"mord\">proj</span></span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.0573em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.4559em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">u</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.3173em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.357em;margin-left:0em;marg